WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.326 --> 00:00:17.270
High above the city, on a tall column, stood the statue of the Happy Prince.

2
00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:24.994
He was gilded all over with thin leaves of fine gold, for eyes he had two bright sapphires,

3
00:00:25.354 --> 00:00:28.656
and a large red ruby glowed on his sword hilt.

4
00:00:29.428 --> 00:00:32.050
He was very much admired indeed.

5
00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:38.896
"He is as beautiful as a weathercock," remarked one of the Town Councilors, who wished to gain a

6
00:00:38.956 --> 00:00:41.698
reputation for having artistic tastes.

7
00:00:41.718 --> 00:00:49.625
"Only not quite so useful," he added, fearing lest people should think him unpractical, which he really was

8
00:00:49.685 --> 00:00:52.788
not. "Why can't you be like the Happy Prince?"

9
00:00:53.628 --> 00:00:57.572
asked a sensible mother of her little boy, who was crying for the moon.

10
00:00:58.328 --> 00:01:01.329
"The Happy Prince never dreams of crying for anything."

11
00:01:01.349 --> 00:01:08.271
"I am glad there is someone in the world who is quite happy," muttered a disappointed man as

12
00:01:08.331 --> 00:01:10.371
he gazed at the wonderful statue.

13
00:01:11.212 --> 00:01:16.813
"He looks just like an angel," said the Charity Children as they came out of the cathedral in

14
00:01:16.853 --> 00:01:20.734
their bright, scarlet cloaks and their clean, white pinafores.

15
00:01:20.754 --> 00:01:22.655
"How do you know?"

16
00:01:23.375 --> 00:01:24.175
said the Master.

17
00:01:24.195 --> 00:01:26.056
"You have never seen one."

18
00:01:26.984 --> 00:01:30.805
"Ah, but we have, in our dreams," answered the children.

19
00:01:31.706 --> 00:01:37.507
And the Master frowned and looked very severe, for he did not approve of children dreaming.

20
00:01:38.588 --> 00:01:42.849
One night, there flew over the city a little Swallow.

21
00:01:43.789 --> 00:01:49.411
His friends had gone away to Egypt six weeks before, but he had stayed behind, for he was

22
00:01:49.631 --> 00:01:52.112
in love with the most beautiful Reed.

23
00:01:53.056 --> 00:01:57.839
He had met her early in the spring as he was flying down the river after a big

24
00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:04.664
yellow moth, and had been so attracted by her slender waist that he had stopped to talk to

25
00:02:04.704 --> 00:02:06.746
her. "Shall I love you?"

26
00:02:07.886 --> 00:02:12.370
said the Swallow, who liked to come to the point at once, and the Reed made him a

27
00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:13.130
low bow.

28
00:02:14.251 --> 00:02:21.336
So he flew round and round her, touching the water with his wings and making silver ripples.

29
00:02:22.432 --> 00:02:26.214
This was his courtship, and it lasted all through the summer.

30
00:02:26.234 --> 00:02:30.896
"It is a ridiculous attachment," twittered the other Swallows.

31
00:02:30.916 --> 00:02:39.199
"She has no money and far too many relations"; and indeed the river was quite full of Reeds.

32
00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:44.061
Then, when the autumn came, they all flew away.

33
00:02:44.982 --> 00:02:51.308
After they had gone, he felt lonely and began to tire of his ladylove.

34
00:02:51.328 --> 00:02:57.951
"She has no conversation," he said, "and I am afraid that she is always flirting with the wind."

35
00:02:58.991 --> 00:03:04.533
And certainly, whenever the wind blew, the Reed made the most graceful curtsies.

36
00:03:04.553 --> 00:03:14.537
"I admit that she is domestic," he continued, "but I love traveling, and my wife, consequently, should love

37
00:03:14.617 --> 00:03:15.798
traveling also."

38
00:03:15.818 --> 00:03:18.479
"Will you come away with me?"

39
00:03:19.179 --> 00:03:22.697
he said finally to her; but the Reed shook her head.

40
00:03:23.358 --> 00:03:25.139
She was so attached to her home.

41
00:03:25.159 --> 00:03:29.021
"You have been trifling with me," he cried.

42
00:03:29.041 --> 00:03:31.423
"I am off to the Pyramids.

43
00:03:32.083 --> 00:03:34.565
Good-bye!" and he flew away.

44
00:03:35.526 --> 00:03:40.129
All day long, he flew, and at nighttime, he arrived at the city.

45
00:03:40.849 --> 00:03:42.190
"Where shall I put up?"

46
00:03:42.911 --> 00:03:43.311
he said.

47
00:03:43.331 --> 00:03:46.473
"I hope the town has made preparations."

48
00:03:47.254 --> 00:03:50.576
Then he saw the statue on the tall column.

49
00:03:51.572 --> 00:03:54.213
"I will put up there," he cried.

50
00:03:54.233 --> 00:03:58.974
"It is a fine position, with plenty of fresh air."

51
00:04:00.034 --> 00:04:03.975
So he alighted just between the feet of the Happy Prince.

52
00:04:03.995 --> 00:04:12.118
"I have a golden bedroom," he said softly to himself as he looked round, and he prepared to

53
00:04:12.158 --> 00:04:19.079
go to sleep; but just as he was putting his head under his wing, a large drop of

54
00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:20.580
water fell on him.

55
00:04:21.496 --> 00:04:22.917
"What a curious thing!"

56
00:04:23.617 --> 00:04:24.217
he cried.

57
00:04:24.237 --> 00:04:31.040
"There is not a single cloud in the sky, the stars are quite clear and bright, and yet

58
00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:32.141
it is raining.

59
00:04:33.041 --> 00:04:35.922
The climate in the north of Europe is really dreadful.

60
00:04:36.783 --> 00:04:41.205
The Reed used to like the rain, but that was merely her selfishness."

61
00:04:42.045 --> 00:04:43.946
Then, another drop fell.

62
00:04:43.966 --> 00:04:47.727
"What is the use of a statue if it cannot keep the rain off?"

63
00:04:48.388 --> 00:04:48.768
he said.

64
00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:53.883
"I must look for a good chimney-pot," and he determined to fly away.

65
00:04:54.784 --> 00:05:01.768
But before he had opened his wings, a third drop fell, and he looked up and saw—Ah!

66
00:05:02.449 --> 00:05:03.269
What did he see?

67
00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:11.174
The eyes of the Happy Prince were filled with tears, and tears were running down his golden cheeks.

68
00:05:11.935 --> 00:05:17.619
His face was so beautiful in the moonlight that the little Swallow was filled with pity.

69
00:05:17.639 --> 00:05:19.400
"Who are you?"

70
00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:20.400
he said.

71
00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:22.921
"I am the Happy Prince."

72
00:05:23.782 --> 00:05:25.082
"Why are you weeping then?"

73
00:05:25.922 --> 00:05:26.843
asked the Swallow.

74
00:05:26.863 --> 00:05:29.064
"You have quite drenched me."

75
00:05:29.844 --> 00:05:37.067
"When I was alive and had a human heart," answered the statue, "I did not know what tears

76
00:05:37.167 --> 00:05:43.970
were, for I lived in the Palace of Sans-Souci, where sorrow is not allowed to enter.

77
00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:51.966
In the daytime, I played with my companions in the garden, and in the evening, I led the

78
00:05:52.006 --> 00:05:53.407
dance in the Great Hall.

79
00:05:54.568 --> 00:06:01.132
Round the garden ran a very lofty wall, but I never cared to ask what lay beyond it;

80
00:06:01.853 --> 00:06:04.314
everything about me was so beautiful.

81
00:06:05.455 --> 00:06:13.921
My courtiers called me the Happy Prince, and happy indeed I was, if pleasure be happiness.

82
00:06:14.902 --> 00:06:17.644
So I lived, and so I died.

83
00:06:18.716 --> 00:06:24.679
And now that I am dead, they have set me up here so high that I can see

84
00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:32.143
all the ugliness and all the misery of my city, and though my heart is made of lead,

85
00:06:32.924 --> 00:06:35.165
yet I cannot choose but weep."

86
00:06:35.185 --> 00:06:38.807
"What! Is he not solid gold?"

87
00:06:39.528 --> 00:06:41.028
said the Swallow to himself.

88
00:06:41.849 --> 00:06:45.371
He was too polite to make any personal remarks out loud.

89
00:06:45.391 --> 00:06:56.459
"Far away," continued the statue in a low musical voice, "far away in a little street, there is

90
00:06:56.499 --> 00:06:57.680
a poor house.

91
00:06:58.961 --> 00:07:05.247
One of the windows is open, and through it, I can see a woman seated at a table.

92
00:07:06.007 --> 00:07:14.475
Her face is thin and worn, and she has coarse, red hands, all pricked by the needle, for

93
00:07:14.555 --> 00:07:15.716
she is a seamstress.

94
00:07:16.964 --> 00:07:25.006
She is embroidering passionflowers on a satin gown for the loveliest of the Queen's maids-of-honor to wear at

95
00:07:25.046 --> 00:07:26.427
the next Court Ball.

96
00:07:27.567 --> 00:07:32.429
In a bed in the corner of the room, her little boy is lying ill.

97
00:07:33.409 --> 00:07:36.590
He has a fever and is asking for oranges.

98
00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:42.571
His mother has nothing to give him but river water, so he is crying.

99
00:07:43.512 --> 00:07:51.485
Swallow, Swallow, little Swallow, will you not bring her the ruby out of my sword hilt?

100
00:07:52.506 --> 00:07:56.409
My feet are fastened to this pedestal, and I cannot move."

101
00:07:56.429 --> 00:08:00.332
"I am waited for in Egypt," said the Swallow.

102
00:08:00.352 --> 00:08:06.497
"My friends are flying up and down the Nile and talking to the large lotus flowers.

103
00:08:07.398 --> 00:08:10.881
Soon, they will go to sleep in the tomb of the great King.

104
00:08:11.421 --> 00:08:14.664
The King is there himself in his painted coffin.

105
00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:19.103
He is wrapped in yellow linen and embalmed with spices.

106
00:08:19.883 --> 00:08:26.088
Round his neck is a chain of pale green jade, and his hands are like withered leaves."
